WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.088 --> 00:00:02.671
(gentle music)

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:18.167
- The protection of water
resources and the provision

00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:22.080
of high quality drinking
water is a key priority

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.520
for public health.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.380
The Federal Safe Drinking
Water Act protects the public

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.100
from legacy, well-known contaminants.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.060
However, there are certain
chemicals, which we call

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.550
emerging contaminants,
that might be present

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.320
in our drinking water.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.530
Emerging contaminants
represent a special challenge

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:47.910
because the very limited
availability of information

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.640
regarding them, and most importantly,

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.540
they're not regulated by federal or state.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.830
1.4 dioxane is a stabilizer
that has been used

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:02.160
with many organic solvents
that it is difficult

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.830
to be filtered from the drinking water.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.790
So whatever there was a
chemical spill of solvents,

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.160
the 1.4 dioxane was with them

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.361
and it has been detected
in several Superfund sites

00:01:15.361 --> 00:01:19.590
throughout United States
and internationally.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.580
Superfund sites are areas
that there is a contamination,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.360
a chemical spill or environmental
release of chemicals

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.010
by the chemical industry.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:33.210
We decided to develop a research
center primarily focusing

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.290
on this chemical.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:38.977
The Yale Superfund Research
Center aims to study the role

00:01:38.977 --> 00:01:43.977
of 1.4 dioxane in human health,
as well as better methods

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.230
of detecting it in the
environment and also better ways

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.440
of mitigating that from
the drinking water.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.620
Based on studies, 1.4 dioxane

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.800
is a probable human carcinogen.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.280
Therefore, we are aiming to
study the molecular mechanism

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.280
by which 1.4 dioxane, along
or with its co-contaminants

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.810
may cause cancer and at the
same time, we are aiming

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.110
to develop biomarkers of
exposure to these chemicals.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.320
Since there is no epidemiological studies

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:24.320
confirming the carcinogenicity
of this compound in humans.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.916
Dr. Deziel and her
colleagues are investigating

00:02:27.916 --> 00:02:32.916
the exposure assessment of 1.4
dioxane in human populations.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.920
- Project two is an
exposure assessment study,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.310
which is the study of how
people come into contact

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:44.940
with chemicals as they move
through their everyday lives.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.713
This could be pollutants in
the air that they breathe,

00:02:47.713 --> 00:02:50.340
chemicals in the food they're eating,

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.830
the personal care products they're using,

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.770
or even contaminants in
their drinking water.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.380
It's really important to
understand what people

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.500
are exposed to, 'cause we
have to know who is exposed

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.460
to what amounts.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.610
You know, certain populations
may have higher levels

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.740
of these chemicals in their drinking water

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.070
or in their blood, and that
will help us understand

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:14.520
whether or not they're at
risk for any health problems.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.820
Our study area includes two
counties on Long Island,

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.780
Nassau and Suffolk Counties,
and these two counties are home

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.920
to dozens of state and
federal Superfund sites.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.010
We're going to hundreds
of homes on Long Island

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.280
to interview people about
the consumer products

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.710
they're using, collect
their drinking water

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:38.340
and test it for chemicals,
collect blood samples to look

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.760
for health markers, and
map where people live

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.880
in relation to hazardous waste sites.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.730
Another reason why exposure
assessment is so important

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.430
is because by identifying communities

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.440
with higher levels of exposures,

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.740
that can help direct some
of the interventions.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.950
So, some of the other projects
in this Superfund Center

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:02.250
are developing treatment
devices that could be deployed

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:06.600
in homes that have particularly
high levels of 1.4 dioxane

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.880
or other chemicals in
their drinking water.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.700
- 1.4 dioxane is a very
difficult pollutant

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.170
to treat if it is in
groundwater because it's so tiny

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:21.150
and it loves water, it doesn't
like to stick to the surface.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:24.690
So, because of that property,
the conventional method

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:29.100
to treat pollutants does not
work well for 1.4 dioxane.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.735
We are trying to use electricity

00:04:31.735 --> 00:04:36.735
and electric power is going
to produce oxidation power,

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.780
meaning destruction power.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.930
Destruction of 1.4
dioxane by hydroxy radical

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:46.980
is a proven chemistry but
it is extremely difficult

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.080
to realize in a smaller scale setting.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.930
For in this center what
you are trying to do

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.879
is to make a small modular system.

00:04:53.879 --> 00:04:57.030
For user's point of
view, you just plug in,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.060
connect the pipe, and
it destroys 1.4 dioxane.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.640
That's the goal, and we
are at the initial stage

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.680
of realizing that goal.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:08.280
- So one critically important
aspect of the Superfund Center

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.680
is the community engagement core

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.710
and I'm leading that along
with Adrienne Esposito

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:17.160
who is the Executive
Director of Citizens Campaign

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.160
For the Environment, which
is a large grassroots

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.310
organization based in Long Island

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.200
that has a long history of working

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.190
on drinking water quality
issues in the study area.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.980
It's a very rich partnership

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.400
between the university and the community.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.880
And the community is
directly providing input

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.470
into the science.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.410
So as scientists, we're not
just going into communities

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:45.510
and studying what we think is important

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:48.990
we're asking the people who live there

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.870
what they're concerned about,
and their, their concerns

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:55.530
and ideas are directly
informing our research questions

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:56.550
and our science.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.700
Doing the science is incredibly important,

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:03.480
but once the work is done
and the results are in

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.390
it's really important
to format the results

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.610
and information in a way that's useful

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:11.280
to the community and to policy makers.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.737
- It is very important
that we inform stakeholders

00:06:15.737 --> 00:06:20.010
and legislators regarding toxic limits

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:23.070
of those emerging contaminants
in our drinking water.

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:27.321
So eventually we would
like to inform policy

00:06:27.321 --> 00:06:31.293
on setting up standards
for our drinking water.

